+\subsection{The first integral obstruction and the relative Fermat fibre}
+
+\begin{statement}{F.23}{Proved}{Actual class-five congruences and complete relative Fermat fibres}
+Retain \(G,N_n,\mathcal S_n\) from F.22. Put
+\[
+ C=[X,Y],\quad U=[X,C],\quad V=[Y,C],\quad
+ E_1=[X,U],\quad E_2=[Y,U],\quad E_3=[Y,V]
+\]
+in the free Lie algebra on \(X=\log x,Y=\log y\), and use the
+degree-five basis
+\[
+ (B_1,\ldots,B_6)=
+ ([X,E_1],[Y,E_1],[Y,E_2],[Y,E_3],[C,U],[C,V]).
+                                                               \tag{F.23.1}
+\]
+There are exact central cohomology groups
+\[
+ H^1(G,\mathbb Z_5(5))=\mathbb Z_5,\qquad
+ H^2(G,\mathbb Z_5(5))=(\mathbb Z/5)^2,                         \tag{F.23.2}
+\]
+and restriction identifies the second group with the direct
+sum of its local groups at \(2\) and \(3\).
+
+For any actual class-four cocycle, define its three local
+coordinates \(v_q=(v_{q,1},v_{q,2},v_{q,3})\in\mathbb F_5^3\)
+at \(q=2,3\) by the explicit normalization below.
+Let \(a_q,b_q\in\mathbb F_5\) be its two Kummer valuation
+coordinates at \(q\). The cocycle lifts to class five if and
+only if, at both primes, the following six equations hold:
+\[
+\begin{aligned}
+ a v_1&=a^4b,&
+ b v_1+a v_2&=3b-a^3b^2,&
+ b v_2+a v_3&=2b+a^2b^3,\\
+ b v_3&=3b-ab^4,&
+ a v_2&=a^3b^2,&
+ a v_3&=2a^2b^3 .
+\end{aligned}                                                   \tag{F.23.3}
+\]
+Here \(a=a_q,b=b_q,v=v_q\), and all equations are in
+\(\mathbb F_5\). Their complete solution set is
+\[
+\begin{array}{c|c}
+(a,b)&v\\ \hline
+(0,0)&\text{arbitrary in }\mathbb F_5^3\\
+(a,0),\ a\ne0&(0,0,0)\\
+(0,b),\ b\ne0&(3,2,3)\\
+(a,a),\ a\ne0&(1,1,2)\\
+ab(a-b)\ne0&\text{no solution}.
+\end{array}                                                     \tag{F.23.4}
+\]
+Each nonempty global class-five fibre is a free
+\(\mathbb Z_5^6\)-torsor. In the metabelian quotient,
+the last two equations are omitted, but the solution set
+(F.23.4) is unchanged; its nonempty fibres are
+\(\mathbb Z_5^4\)-torsors. In particular, every class-four
+section above the abelian pair
+\((\kappa(2),2\kappa(2))\) fails to lift, both in the full
+and in the metabelian tower.
+
+All the lower global parameters are retained in this test.
+For a fixed global class-three class, choose any class-four
+lift and write its two normalized local vectors as
+\(v_2^0,v_3^0\). The \(125\) class-four lifts are indexed by
+\(t\in\mathbb F_5^3\), and their local vectors are exactly
+\[
+                       v_2^0+t,\qquad v_3^0+t.                \tag{F.23.5}
+\]
+Thus when both nonzero valuation pairs are among the three
+permitted directions, a class-four lift survives precisely
+when
+\[
+ v_3^0-v_2^0=R(a_3,b_3)-R(a_2,b_2),                          \tag{F.23.6}
+\]
+where \(R\) is the corresponding singleton vector in
+(F.23.4). When exactly one valuation pair is nonzero in a
+permitted direction, exactly one of the \(125\) lifts
+survives. When both valuation pairs are zero, all \(125\)
+survive. The difference on the left of (F.23.6) is independent
+of the chosen class-four origin; its dependence on the full
+global class-three cocycle is not discarded.
+
+In particular, the entire induced relative Fermat tower at
+class five is evaluated. For \(r\geq1\), let
+\[
+ K_{5,r}=\ker\!\left(N_5\longrightarrow
+                              (\mathbb Z/5^r(1))^2\right).
+\]
+After choices of torsor origins,
+\[
+\begin{aligned}
+ H^1(G,K_{5,r})&\simeq
+        (5^r\mathbb Z_5)^6\times\mathbb Z_5^8
+                                      \times(\mathbb Z/5)^3,\\
+ H^1(G,K_{5,r}^{\rm met})&\simeq
+        (5^r\mathbb Z_5)^6\times\mathbb Z_5^6
+                                      \times(\mathbb Z/5)^3.
+\end{aligned}                                                   \tag{F.23.7}
+\]
+The maps to the ambient section sets are injective and
+identify transition maps with inclusion of the indicated
+abelian divisibility loci. The fibre over abelian zero,
+equivalently the classes descending to every relative
+Fermat level, is respectively
+\[
+ \mathbb Z_5^8\oplus(\mathbb Z/5)^3,\qquad
+ \mathbb Z_5^6\oplus(\mathbb Z/5)^3.                           \tag{F.23.8}
+\]
+For this last assertion an explicit integral diagonalization
+of the actual arithmetic action is given in the proof.
+These are induced relative quotients and retain the stated
+finite ramification set.
+\end{statement}
+\begin{proof}
+\emph{The arithmetic obstruction group and its local detection.}
+First replace \(S\) by \(S_0=\{5\}\). Over
+\(\mathbb Q(\zeta_5)\), the ring with just the prime over
+five inverted has trivial ideal class group and trivial
+Brauer group: in the global Brauer sequence there is only
+one finite place and no real place. The argument of F.22
+therefore gives
+\[
+ H^2(G_{\mathbb Q,S_0},\mathbb Z_5(j))=0\quad(j\geq1).
+                                                               \tag{F.23.9}
+\]
+At weight five, the \(S_0\)-unit module modulo fifth powers
+has characters \(\mathbf1\oplus\omega\oplus\omega^2\).
+Twisting by \(\omega^4=1\) selects one dimension.
+The torsion invariant
+\(H^0(G_{\mathbb Q,S_0},\mathbb Q_5/\mathbb Z_5(5))\)
+is zero. The same compact Nakayama argument as in F.22
+gives \(H^1(G_{\mathbb Q,S_0},\mathbb Z_5(5))=\mathbb Z_5\).
+
+The localization sequence on \(\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z[1/5]\)
+when \(2,3\) are deleted has residue terms
+\(H^{i-1}(\mathbb F_q,\mathbb Z_5(4))\).
+For completeness the codimension-one purity calculation
+needed here is the elementary one in
+\citet[II, \S0.1(f), Corollary~1.6 and Remark~1.7(b)]{FA_Milne2006ADT}:
+\(R^2i^!\mu_{5^r}=\mathbb Z/5^r\), with the other
+cohomology sheaves zero. Tensoring by the unramified
+fourth twist gives the displayed residue terms.
+Passing to integral coefficients is exact because every
+finite-coefficient group in this fixed finite set is finite.
+The residue-field invariants in the integral module
+\(\mathbb Z_5(4)\) are zero, and
+\[
+ H^1(\mathbb F_q,\mathbb Z_5(4))
+           =\mathbb Z_5/(q^4-1).
+\]
+Also \(H^3(G_{\mathbb Q,S_0},\mathbb Z_5(5))=0\), by odd
+cohomological dimension two and the same inverse-limit
+argument. Thus localization gives (F.23.2), since
+\(v_5(2^4-1)=v_5(3^4-1)=1\), and does not change \(H^1\).
+The local inertia--residue exact sequence identifies
+\(H^2(\mathbb Q_q,\mathbb Z_5(5))\) with this same residue
+group. This proves that the two local restrictions detect
+the entire global obstruction. No claim that the entire
+group \(K_8(\mathbb Z)\) is known to vanish is needed.
+
+\emph{The actual Frobenius action through the required degree.}
+Fix tame inertia \(\tau_q\) and arithmetic Frobenius
+\(\phi_q\), so that
+\(\phi_q\tau_q\phi_q^{-1}=\tau_q^q\).
+The geometric arithmetic action is unramified at \(q=2,3\).
+Write the logarithm of its actual associator as
+\[
+ \log f_{\phi_q}
+ =r_qC+h_q(U+V)+c_q(E_1+E_3)+d_qE_2
+                                      \pmod{\text{degree }5}.
+                                                               \tag{F.23.10}
+\]
+The two-cycle gives the indicated equal coefficients;
+the class-two hexagon in E.16 gives
+\(r_q=(q^2-1)/24\).
+The degree-three coefficient \(h_q\) is retained. It is
+the first Soulé parameter, up to its stated normalization
+and possible coboundary, not a zero coefficient assumed
+from a graded model.
+
+The actual pentagon forces the following exact values:
+\[
+\begin{array}{c|rrr}
+ q&r_q&c_q&d_q\\ \hline
+ 2&1/8&-1/96&-1/128\\
+ 3&1/3&-1/18&-1/36.
+\end{array}                                                     \tag{F.23.11}
+\]
+Here is an explicit certificate for this step.
+Use the action of \(PB_4\) on its free fibre with generators
+\(u=x_{14},v=x_{24},w=x_{34}\), as in the presentation
+checked in E.17. For \(A=x_{12},B=x_{23},D=x_{13}\),
+the conjugations \(g^{-1}(\,\cdot\,)g\) are
+\[
+\begin{array}{c|ccc}
+ &u&v&w\\ \hline
+ A&uvu v^{-1}u^{-1}&uvu^{-1}&w\\
+ B&u&vwv w^{-1}v^{-1}&vwv^{-1}\\
+ D&uwu w^{-1}u^{-1}&[u,w]v[u,w]^{-1}&uwu^{-1}.
+\end{array}                                                     \tag{F.23.12}
+\]
+The fibre generators act by their own inner conjugations.
+The program verifies the nine displayed fibre relations
+and the three centrality relations for \(ADB\), and
+evaluates the actual group pentagon, with its five
+insertion/cabling maps, in this representation.
+Faithfulness is unnecessary: every genuine pentagon
+must hold in this representation.
+
+In the image of the generator \(u\), the coefficients
+of the Magnus words \(u^3vw\) and \(u^3wv\), where these
+letters now denote the augmentation variables, give
+the two equations
+\[
+ 3c_q-2d_q=-r_q^2,\qquad
+       -c_q-d_q=r_q/12+r_q^2/2.                              \tag{F.23.13}
+\]
+Their coefficient determinant is \(-5\).
+All remaining associative coefficients of the pentagon
+and hexagon are checked by exact rational arithmetic.
+Terms in \(h_q\) enter affinely and cancel; products of
+a degree-three term with a degree-two term first occur
+beyond the degree being checked. Solving (F.23.13) in
+\(\mathbb Q_5\), then checking the resulting denominators
+are five-adic units for these two \(q\), proves (F.23.11).
+The nonunit determinant is kept in the certificate.
+
+The parameter \(h_q\) can now be removed \emph{locally by
+an integral change of geometric coordinates}.
+Let \(\eta_q\) fix \(x\) and send
+\[
+ y\longmapsto k_q^{-1}yk_q,\qquad
+ \log k_q=-\frac{h_q}{q^3-1}(U+V).
+                                                               \tag{F.23.14}
+\]
+These are integral elements through class five:
+\(q^3-1\) is a five-adic unit, and the logarithm of the
+subgroup \(\gamma_3N_5\) has only five-integral
+denominators, as checked explicitly below.
+Conjugating the action by \(\eta_q\) in the form
+\(\eta_q^{-1}\rho(\phi_q)\eta_q\) makes \(h_q=0\) in
+(F.23.10) and preserves \(r_q,c_q,d_q\).
+The program checks this exact equality through class
+five; degree considerations make it an affine identity
+in \(h_q\). The section cocycle is transported by
+\(\eta_q^{-1}\) at the same time. Thus its arithmetic
+information has been retained in its coordinates.
+
+\emph{Definition of the local coordinates and the twelve equations.}
+After (F.23.14), conjugate a local class-four cocycle
+so that its value on \(\phi_q\) has no components of
+degrees one, two or three. This is possible successively
+and uniquely in those degrees because \(q^j-1\) is a
+five-adic unit for \(j=1,2,3\).
+Its remaining value is in the central fourth layer;
+write its logarithm there as \(W_q=\sum_i w_{q,i}E_i\).
+Changing the fourth component of the conjugating element
+changes \(W_q\) by \((q^4-1)L_4\). Therefore
+\[
+ v_q=\left(\frac{q^4-1}{5}\right)^{-1}
+                    (w_{q,1},w_{q,2},w_{q,3})\pmod5          \tag{F.23.15}
+\]
+is well defined for the local section class and the
+declared geometric coordinates.
+
+Its inertia value \(I_q\in N_4\) has abelianization
+\(aX+bY\), with the integral Kummer valuations \(a,b\).
+Because the Frobenius value is central in \(N_4\),
+the tame cocycle relation is
+\[
+                   \rho(\phi_q)(I_q)=I_q^q\quad\text{in }N_4.
+                                                               \tag{F.23.16}
+\]
+For any given \(a,b\in\mathbb Z_5\), this determines the
+inertia value uniquely: in degrees \(j=2,3,4\), correcting
+the \(j\)th Hall coordinate multiplies its defect by
+\(q^j-q\), which is a five-adic unit.
+The program implements exactly this recursive solution,
+starting with \(x^ay^b\), and retains all the resulting
+Hall coordinates.
+
+Lift the resulting word to \(N_5\) with its six fifth
+Hall coordinates set to zero. The fifth-degree defect
+of (F.23.16), divided by \(q(q^4-1)/5\), is
+\[
+ P(a,b)=
+ (a^4b,\ 3b-a^3b^2,\ 2b+a^2b^3,\ 3b-ab^4,\
+                                  a^3b^2,\ 2a^2b^3)
+                      \quad\text{in }\mathbb F_5^6.           \tag{F.23.17}
+\]
+This is an actual Magnus calculation, with the coefficients
+(F.23.11) inserted. It uses group products and power series
+with integral coefficients, rather than applying a
+class-less-than-five BCH correspondence in class five.
+The first four Hall-coordinate corrections have denominators
+prime to five. Dependence on the abelian exponents modulo
+five is sufficient: modulo five, replacing \(x^a\) by
+\(x^{a+5}\) changes the Magnus series first by the central
+degree-five term \(X^5\); its contributions to the two
+sides of (F.23.16) are \(q^5X^5\) and \(qX^5\), which
+agree modulo five. The same holds for \(Y^5\); fifth
+powers of commutators first occur in degree at least ten.
+Thus all \(25\) residue pairs exhaust this calculation.
+The exact program verifies (F.23.17) on each pair for
+both Frobenius values.
+
+Restoring the fourth-layer Frobenius value contributes
+the commutator \(q[W_q,aX+bY]\) to the defect. In the basis
+(F.23.1), its negative, after the same normalization, is
+\[
+ (av_1,\ bv_1+av_2,\ bv_2+av_3,\ bv_3,\ av_2,\ av_3).
+                                                               \tag{F.23.18}
+\]
+These formulas follow directly from Jacobi:
+\([X,E_2]=B_2+B_5\) and \([X,E_3]=B_3+B_6\).
+The local obstruction is therefore exactly
+(F.23.3). Changing the fifth-layer inertia lift alters
+the integral defect by \((q^5-q)L_5\); changing the
+central Frobenius lift has no effect. Since
+\(v_5(q^5-q)=1\), vanishing of the displayed residue
+is necessary and sufficient for a local lift.
+Wild inertia and the other prime-primary tame factors
+have trivial maps into the pro-five target. Conversely
+the tame relation suffices, since the Frobenius and
+inertia values then define a continuous cocycle on the
+tame local quotient.
+
+The solution table (F.23.4) can also be checked without
+enumeration. On the two coordinate axes the equations
+immediately give the displayed vectors. If \(ab\ne0\),
+the first, fifth and sixth equations give
+\(v_1=a^3b,v_2=a^2b^2,v_3=2ab^3\).
+The second then forces \(a^3b=1\), hence \(a=b\) in
+\(\mathbb F_5^\times\), and the remaining equations hold.
+For the metabelian quotient, \(B_5,B_6\) vanish.
+Solving just the first four equations when \(ab\ne0\)
+reduces, with \(t=b/a\), to
+\((t+1)^3=3\). Cubing permutes \(\mathbb F_5^\times\);
+the unique solution is \(t=1\).
+The exact enumeration gives \(125+12=137\) permitted
+parameter pairs out of \(25\cdot125=3125\) at each prime,
+for both the full and metabelian calculation.
+
+By the localization isomorphism already proved, these
+two local tests are the complete global obstruction test.
+The fixed-group argument of F.22 works integrally in
+every finite lower-central class, including the twisted
+class-four base. Thus nonempty fibres are free torsors
+under \(H^1(G,L_5)\), of ranks six and four as claimed.
+The example \((\kappa(2),2\kappa(2))\) has residue pair
+\((1,2)\) at \(2\), which fails (F.23.4), independently
+of all of its higher choices.
+
+\emph{The common shift and the retained global parameters.}
+For a fixed global class-three class the \(125\) lifts
+are exactly the twists by
+\(z_4(g)\sum_i t_iE_i\) of F.22. At \(\phi_q\) this
+adds \((q^4-1)t/5\). The change (F.23.14) acts trivially
+on the fourth layer, and normalization in lower degrees
+is unchanged. Hence (F.23.15) changes by the same vector
+\(t\) at both primes. This proves (F.23.5)--(F.23.6).
+In particular the complete global class-three parameters
+are retained inside \(v_3^0-v_2^0\); independently
+normalizing both local vectors to zero would change
+the global problem and is not done.
+
+When all six abelian coordinates are divisible by
+\(5^r\), \(r\geq1\), both residue pairs are zero.
+Every class-four section in this relative Fermat image
+therefore lifts. The injection and image argument for
+the Fermat kernels in F.22 applies unchanged to \(N_5\).
+It yields (F.23.7), including the transition maps.
+
+\emph{An integral diagonalization of the entire abelian-zero fibre.}
+The subgroup \(M=\gamma_3N_5\) is abelian, since brackets
+of two third-layer elements have degree at least six.
+Its logarithm is the integral lattice
+\[
+                          L_3\oplus L_4\oplus L_5.
+                                                               \tag{F.23.19}
+\]
+This does not assert an integral BCH correspondence for
+all of \(N_5\). To verify (F.23.19), take the two third,
+three fourth and six fifth Hall commutators specified
+above. Their logarithms, in increasing weights, form a
+unipotent triangular change of basis whose entries have
+denominators prime to five. Products in \(M\) add their
+logarithms. The exact \(11\)-by-\(11\) change-of-basis
+matrix is retained in the certificate. Thus this
+particular abelian subgroup has the stated integral
+logarithm lattice even in class five.
+
+For arbitrary \(g\in G\), put \(\lambda=\chi_5(g)\).
+Only the quadratic term
+\((\lambda^2-1)C/24\) of \(\log f_g\) acts nontrivially
+on \(L_3\) through degree five. Define the degree-two
+derivation
+\[
+ D_2(X)=0,\qquad D_2(Y)=[Y,C].
+\]
+Then for \(v\in L_3\),
+\[
+ \rho(g)v=\lambda^3
+       \left(v+\frac{\lambda^2-1}{24}D_2(v)\right),
+ \qquad
+ \rho(g)|_{L_4}=\lambda^4,\quad
+ \rho(g)|_{L_5}=\lambda^5.                                   \tag{F.23.20}
+\]
+This follows by expanding the actual action
+\(\rho(g)Y=\exp(-\operatorname{ad}\log f_g)(\lambda Y)\).
+In particular
+\[
+ D_2(U)=B_2+B_5,\qquad D_2(V)=B_3-B_6.
+\]
+The integral corrected basis
+\[
+        \widetilde U=U-(B_2+B_5)/24,\qquad
+        \widetilde V=V-(B_3-B_6)/24                           \tag{F.23.21}
+\]
+therefore transforms by \(\lambda^3\). This proves the
+actual \(G\)-module isomorphism
+\[
+ M\simeq \mathbb Z_5(3)^2\oplus
+          \mathbb Z_5(4)^3\oplus\mathbb Z_5(5)^6 .
+                                                               \tag{F.23.22}
+\]
+The unknown higher Soulé coefficients have degree too
+large to affect this calculation; they have not been
+assigned artificial values in the action.
+In the metabelian quotient discard \(B_5,B_6\), giving
+four last summands.
+
+Since \(H^1(G,N_2)\to H^1(G,N_1)\) is a bijection and
+every twisted \(N_2\) has trivial fixed group,
+\(H^1(G,M)\to H^1(G,N_5)\) is injective with image
+exactly the abelian-zero fibre. Taking cohomology in
+(F.23.22), using F.22 and (F.23.2), proves (F.23.8).
+For chosen integral cocycles \(\eta_3,\eta_5\) generating
+the two cyclic free cohomology groups, its elements
+are explicitly represented by
+\[
+ \exp\!\left(
+ \eta_3(g)(s_1\widetilde U+s_2\widetilde V)
+  +z_4(g)\sum_{i=1}^3t_iE_i
+  +\eta_5(g)\sum_{j=1}^6r_jB_j\right),
+                                                               \tag{F.23.23}
+\]
+where \(s_i,r_j\in\mathbb Z_5\) and \(0\leq t_i<5\).
+The exponential in this formula is the integral
+isomorphism for the abelian group \(M\).
+
+The finite certificate supplies all polynomial identities,
+the nonzero pentagon minor, local coordinate changes,
+all \(6250\) local parameter tests, and the integral
+matrix in (F.23.19). Formula (F.23.6) remains expressed
+in the actual localized global cocycles: a separate
+closed formula for that frame difference in arbitrary
+chosen global torsor coordinates has not been asserted.
+The explicit no-lift example and the entire relative
+Fermat calculation do not require such a formula.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{statement}{F.24}{Proved}{The unit cusps first separate at integral class three}
+For the actual pro-five action, the three unit tangential
+sections at \(0+1\), \(1-1\), and the image of \(0+1\)
+under \(z\mapsto1/z\) have the same class-one and
+class-two classes, but are pairwise distinct in class
+three. This holds over the finite ramification set
+\(S=\{2,3,5\}\), and also after inflation to \(G_{\mathbb Q}\).
+All three classes descend to every induced relative
+Fermat level, as described in F.22--F.23.
+
+More precisely, use the standard unit-interval path
+and the logarithmic path convention of
+\citet[\S2.1, (2.4), Proposition~2.2 and Lemma~2.4]{FA_NakamuraSakugawaWojtkowiak2017}.
+For \(\sigma\in\ker\chi_5\), its path cocycle satisfies
+\[
+       (\log f_\sigma)_3
+            =\frac{\kappa_3(\sigma)}{48}
+                         [X+Y,[X,Y]],                       \tag{F.24.1}
+\]
+where \(\kappa_3\) is the raw cyclotomic Soulé character
+defined by the Kummer products in that source.
+Its reduction modulo five is nonzero; in fact
+\(\kappa_3\) has image \(\mathbb Z_5\) on the
+cyclotomic kernel. The path convention is part of
+(F.24.1); reversing a transition path changes the
+sign of its first nonzero term and does not affect
+the separation assertion.
+\end{statement}
+\begin{proof}
+The class-two collision was proved in F.20. We now
+verify an integral nonzero third-layer term, including
+its normalization and nonvanishing.
+
+Put \(L=\mathbb Q(\zeta_5)\), and
+\(\varepsilon=(1+\sqrt5)/2\).
+The first Kummer product for \(\kappa_3\) is
+\[
+               \epsilon_{3,1}
+                   =\prod_{a=1}^4(1-\zeta_5^a)^{a^2}.
+\]
+Modulo fifth powers in \(L^\times\), pair the factors
+with indices \(a\) and \(5-a\) to obtain
+\[
+\begin{split}
+ \epsilon_{3,1}
+ &\equiv
+ \bigl((1-\zeta_5)(1-\zeta_5^{-1})\bigr)
+ \bigl((1-\zeta_5^2)(1-\zeta_5^{-2})\bigr)^4\\
+ &=\left(\frac{\sqrt5}{\varepsilon}\right)
+                   (\sqrt5\,\varepsilon)^4
+   =(\sqrt5)^5\varepsilon^3
+                 \pmod{L^{\times5}}.                        \tag{F.24.2}
+\end{split}
+\]
+The unit \(\varepsilon\) is not a fifth power in \(L\).
+Indeed it is the fundamental positive unit of
+\(\mathbb Q(\sqrt5)\). One can verify the latter
+elementary claim directly: a positive unit strictly
+between \(1\) and \(\varepsilon\) would have integral
+trace strictly between \(0\) and \(1\) if its norm is
+\(-1\), or strictly between \(2\) and \(3\) if its norm
+is \(1\). The rank-one unit theorem then makes
+\(\varepsilon\) a generator. If \(u^5=\varepsilon\)
+in \(L\), taking the norm to its real subfield would
+give \(N(u)^5=\varepsilon^2\), which is impossible
+in that unit group.
+
+Since \(L\) contains \(\mu_5\), Kummer theory now gives
+\([L(\varepsilon^{1/5}):L]=5\).
+Furthermore \(L(\varepsilon^{1/5})\) is not contained
+in \(\mathbb Q(\mu_{5^\infty})\). It is Galois over
+\(\mathbb Q\): the nontrivial real-subfield
+automorphism sends \(\varepsilon\) to
+\(-\varepsilon^{-1}\), and \(-1\) has a fifth root
+in \(\mathbb Q\). Complex conjugation fixes a real
+fifth root of \(\varepsilon\), but inverts the
+nontrivial Kummer group of order five. Thus this
+Galois extension is nonabelian, whereas every finite
+subextension of the cyclotomic extension is abelian.
+Consequently (F.24.2) gives a nonzero Kummer character
+even after restriction to the full cyclotomic kernel.
+The defining congruences for the Soulé character,
+at \(n=1\), identify it with the reduction of
+\(\kappa_3\) modulo five. This proves the asserted
+nonzero reduction. Its compact image is a closed
+subgroup of \(\mathbb Z_5\) containing a unit, hence
+is all of \(\mathbb Z_5\).
+
+For the coefficient in (F.24.1), the cited source
+defines the path cochain by
+\(\gamma=f_\sigma*\sigma(\gamma)\) and writes
+\[
+ \log f_\sigma\equiv-\kappa_z(\sigma)X
+       -\sum_{m\geq1}\ell i_m(z)(\sigma)
+                         \operatorname{ad}_X^{m-1}Y
+\]
+in the quotient retaining words with at most one \(Y\).
+Although its main application is local, the paragraph
+following Proposition~2.2 explicitly gives the same
+Kummer measure over number fields, and (2.11)
+specializes it to \(F=\mathbb Q,z=1\). Thus the
+identity used here is an identity on the global
+cyclotomic kernel.
+At the unit tangent \(z=1\), Proposition~2.2 and
+Lemma~2.4 give, on the cyclotomic kernel,
+\[
+ \ell i_3(1)=\frac{\widetilde\kappa_3}{2},
+ \qquad
+ \widetilde\kappa_3=\frac{\kappa_3}{1-5^2}.
+                                                               \tag{F.24.3}
+\]
+The same paper's (2.11) explicitly identifies this
+restricted Kummer character with the usual Soulé
+character when \(z=1\). Equations (F.24.3) make the
+coefficient of \([X,[X,Y]]\) in \(\log f_\sigma\)
+equal to \(\kappa_3/48\).
+The two-cycle exchanges \(X\) and \(Y\) and forces
+the same coefficient on \([Y,[X,Y]]\), proving
+(F.24.1). This direct path computation also agrees
+with the independent Tate substitution in
+\citet[Theorems~5.2.4--5.2.6]{Nakamura2013Eisenstein}.
+
+For \(\sigma\in\ker\chi_5\), the quadratic associator
+coefficient vanishes by E.16. Thus \(f_\sigma\in\gamma_3P\),
+and the actual arithmetic action on \(N_3\) is trivial:
+conjugating a degree-one generator by \(f_\sigma\)
+first changes degree four. A coboundary on \(G\),
+when restricted to this subgroup, must therefore
+be the identity. The transition cocycle between the
+two unit tangents has the nonzero restriction
+(F.24.1), so their class-three section classes
+cannot be equal. The same argument works over
+\(G_{\mathbb Q}\), since the Kummer extension used
+above is unramified away from five.
+
+Finally the rational \(S_3\)-symmetry of the tripod
+acts on arithmetic section classes and permutes the
+three cusps. On the chosen unit tangents it changes
+a tangent parameter at most by a sign.
+Changing a cusp tangent by \(-1\) does not change its
+pro-five section class: its Kummer class is zero,
+as the compatible fifth-power roots of \(-1\)
+can all be chosen to be \(-1\) in \(\mathbb Q\).
+The three cusp packets are consequently permuted
+transitively on ordered pairs. Separation of the
+first two proves separation of every pair.
+Their abelian Kummer coordinates are all zero, so
+the relative Fermat descent assertions of F.22--F.23
+apply to each of them.
+
+The certificate for F.23 also verifies the exact
+cyclotomic identity (F.24.2), retaining a fifth-power
+factor rather than only a numerical congruence.
+For comparison, it records
+\(\epsilon_{5,1}\equiv5\pmod{L^{\times5}}\).
+Here \(5\) is not a fifth power in \(L\), since its
+valuation at the prime over five is \(4\).
+The same real Kummer and cyclotomic argument shows
+that the raw \(\kappa_5\) is primitive. Reindexing
+the defining Kummer product by multiplication by
+\(\chi_5(g)\) gives
+\(\kappa_m(g\sigma g^{-1})=\chi_5(g)^m\kappa_m(\sigma)\)
+for \(m=3,5\) and \(\sigma\in\ker\chi_5\).
+These characters are unramified away from five,
+since all the defining factors are cyclotomic
+five-units and their Kummer extensions are obtained
+by adjoining fifth-power roots. The characters
+\(\kappa_3,\kappa_5\) extend uniquely as cohomology
+classes in \(H^1(G_{\mathbb Q,\{5\}},\mathbb Z_5(3))\)
+and \(H^1(G_{\mathbb Q,\{5\}},\mathbb Z_5(5))\):
+in inflation--restriction the cyclotomic quotient
+has zero \(H^1\) and \(H^2\) with either coefficient,
+because its order-four subgroup acts nontrivially.
+They can therefore be used for the primitive
+generators \(\eta_3,\eta_5\) in (F.23.23), with
+the indicated explicit normalization.
+\end{proof}
